lysis:**\n- I have 19,950 chips (essentially full stack)\n- This is hand 14/20, so we're in the later stages\n- I'm down 750 chips cumulatively, meaning I need to win more to catch up\n- Pre-flop with no community cards shown yet\n\n**Hand Strength:**\nAQ offsuit is strong enough to:\n- Dominate most Ace-x hands (AJ, AT, A9, etc.)\n- Beat any Queen-x hand\n- Only behind AA, KK, QQ, and AK (roughly 4-5% of hands)\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n1. **Check**: Too passive with a premium hand. Gives opponent free cards and doesn't build the pot when I likely have the best hand.\n\n2. **Raise 200 (min-raise to 300 total)**: Standard play. Builds the pot, gets value from worse hands, and allows me to see opponent's reaction. Keeps pot manageable while maintaining fold equity.\n\n3. **Larger Raise (400-600)**: More aggressive, but might fold out hands I want to keep in. Could be good for protection.\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Extremely aggressive pre-flop. Only makes sense if I think opponent will call with worse hands often, but this risks my entire stack unnecessarily when I can play postflop with position.\n\n5. **Fold (-1)**: Terrible option with AQ - this is way too strong to fold pre-flop in heads-up.\n\nGiven I'm behind in chips cumulatively and need to start accumulating, I should be aggressive but not reckless. A standard raise to build the pot is optimal.\n\nReason: \nAQ is a premium heads-up hand that performs well against most ranges. A standard raise of 200 (to 300 total) accomplishes multiple goals: it builds the pot with a strong hand, allows me to gather information about my opponent's hand strength based on their response, and keeps the pot size manageable for post-flop play. Since I need to catch up in chips but still have 6 hands remaining, I don't need to take excessive risks with an all-in pre-flop. A measured approach maximizes my expected value.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
